## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical string data-struct understanding: duplicates, memory use, and prefix-based queries via a Trie vs simpler maps.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node passCount and endCount
- Duplicate handling via endCount; memory managed by lazy pruning
- Time complexity O(|word|) insert/delete and O(|prefix|) query

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.pass = 0;
    this.end = 0;
  }
}

class WordDict {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass++;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.pass++;
    }
    node.end++;
  }
  delete(word) {
    if (!this._exists(word)) return false;
    let node = this.root;
    node.pass--;
    for (const ch of word) {
      const next = node.children.get(ch);
      next.pass--;
      if (next.pass === 0) { node.children.delete(ch); return true; }
      node = next;
    }
    node.end--;
    return true;
  }
  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.pass;
  }
  _exists(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.end > 0;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support bulk insertions and ensure amortized cleanup of unused nodes?
- How would you adapt for case-insensitive matching and Unicode words?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compact, eviction-aware data structure for time-windowed counting, a common pattern in real-time analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window eviction via timestamped events
- Bucketed counts: map from count to set of users for fast top-k
- Invariants: update counts atomically during insertion and eviction
- Complexity: O(log n) updates, O(k log n) top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.count = new Map(); // user -> cnt
    this.buckets = new Map(); // cnt -> Set<user>
    this.events = new Deque(); // (ts, user)
  }
  _addToBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) this.buckets.set(cnt, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(cnt).add(user);
  }
  _removeFromBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) return;
    this.buckets.get(cnt).delete(user);
    if (this.buckets.get(cnt).size === 0) this.buckets.delete(cnt);
  }
  prune(now) {
    while (this.events.length && this.events.peek().ts <= now - this.W) {
      const {ts, user} = this.events.shift();
      const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
      if (c > 0) {
        this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
        const nc = c - 1;
        if (nc > 0) {
          this._addToBucket(nc, user);
          this.count.set(user, nc);
        } else {
          this.count.delete(user);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  addEvent(user, ts) {
    this.events.push({ts, user});
    this.prune(ts);
    const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
    if (c > 0) this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
    const nc = c + 1;
    this._addToBucket(nc, user);
    this.count.set(user, nc);
  }
  topK(now, k) {
    const res = [];
    // iterate counts from high to low
    const counts = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    for (const cnt of counts) {
      const set = this.buckets.get(cnt);
      const users = Array.from(set).sort((a,b)=>a-b); // tie-breaker by userId
      for (const u of users) {
        res.push([u, cnt]);
        if (res.length === k) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support multiple simultaneous windows (e.g., W1 and W2) efficiently?
- How would you handle very large userId spaces with memory constraints, or concurrent updates in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model simple state transitions and undo/redo semantics with basic data structures.

## Key Concepts

- History management with stacks
- Amortized O(1) operations
- Edge cases: empty history, initial page

## Code Example

```javascript
class BrowserHistory {
  constructor(startUrl) {
    this.back = [];
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = startUrl ?? null;
  }
  visit(url) {
    if (this.currentUrl !== null) this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
    this.forward = [];
    this.currentUrl = url;
  }
  back(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.back.length) {
      this.forward.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.back.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  forward(steps) {
    while (steps > 0 && this.forward.length) {
      this.back.push(this.currentUrl);
      this.currentUrl = this.forward.pop();
      steps--;
    }
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
  current() {
    return this.currentUrl;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale to multiple tabs with independent histories?
- How would you extend to persist history across sessions without refetching pages?

