## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a mutable deque with constant-time min/max queries, a common interview pattern for surface-level data-structure mastery.

## Key Concepts
- Deque operations and invariants
- Two-stack technique for amortized efficiency
- Maintaining per-element min/max for O(1) queries
- Edge-case handling (empty, duplicates)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxDeque {
  constructor() {
    this.front = [];
    this.back = [];
  }
  pushFront(x) {
    const last = this.front[this.front.length - 1];
    const mn = last ? Math.min(x, last.min) : x;
    const mx = last ? Math.max(x, last.max) : x;
    this.front.push({ v: x, min: mn, max: mx });
  }
  pushBack(x) {
    const last = this.back[this.back.length - 1];
    const mn = last ? Math.min(x, last.min) : x;
    const mx = last ? Math.max(x, last.max) : x;
    this.back.push({ v: x, min: mn, max: mx });
  }
  popFront() {
    if (this.front.length === 0) {
      while (this.back.length) this.pushFront(this.back.pop().v);
    }
    if (this.front.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.front.pop().v;
  }
  popBack() {
    if (this.back.length === 0) {
      while (this.front.length) this.pushBack(this.front.pop().v);
    }
    if (this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.back.pop().v;
  }
  getMin() {
    if (this.front.length === 0 && this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    let mn = null;
    if (this.front.length) mn = this.front[this.front.length - 1].min;
    if (this.back.length) {
      const bmin = this.back[this.back.length - 1].min;
      mn = mn === null ? bmin : Math.min(mn, bmin);
    }
    return mn;
  }
  getMax() {
    if (this.front.length === 0 && this.back.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    let mx = null;
    if (this.front.length) mx = this.front[this.front.length - 1].max;
    if (this.back.length) {
      const bmax = this.back[this.back.length - 1].max;
      mx = mx === null ? bmax : Math.max(mx, bmax);
    }
    return mx;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support getMinAtMost(k) for the k smallest elements in the deque?
- Analyze worst-case memory when all pushes happen on one end, and suggest optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked

Autocomplete-like prefix queries with dynamic updates are common in databases and search systems. A Trie with counts cleanly supports insertions, deletions, and fast prefix queries, even with duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node pass-through counts
- O(len(word)) insertion/erasure; O(len(prefix)) prefix queries
- Correct handling of duplicates via counts, not just terminal markers

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
    this.end = false;
  }
}

class WordTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }

  insertWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt++;
    }
    node.end = true;
  }

  eraseWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt--;
    }
    node.end = false;
  }

  countWordsWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    return node.cnt;
  }
}
```

## How It Works

- **insertWord**: Traverses/creates nodes for each character, incrementing the pass-through count at each node
- **eraseWord**: Traverses existing nodes, decrementing counts; nodes with zero count can be pruned
- **countWordsWithPrefix**: Traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass-through count, representing all words that pass through this prefix

The pass-through count at each node represents how many words in the trie have that prefix, enabling O(len(prefix)) prefix queries while maintaining O(len(word)) updates.

